window.docContents[22].resolve({"/Maps/#section-morphisms":{"contents":"Graph homomorphisms, embeddings, and isomorphisms are the structure-preserving maps\nbetween graphs. Unlike SimpleGraph.comap, which takes a plain function and builds a\nnew graph, a morphism is data relating two given graphs: a function between their\nvertex types together with a proof that it respects adjacency. We will see below how\nthe two ideas are related.\n\nBefore looking at morphisms in detail we note that they are all defined as abbrevs\nfor the corresponding Rel* types in Mathlib. This means that occasionally when\nworking with these maps we will use results that refer to RelHom, RelEmbedding or\nRelIso.\n\nLeft: a homomorphism from C_4 onto K_2, identifying opposite vertices.Centre: an embedding of a path. Right: an isomorphism\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Maps","header":"5.3. Morphisms","id":"/Maps/#section-morphisms"}});